california is moving ahead with its  long-planned high-speed rail project  that one day may connect san francisco  to los angeles that's not going to  happen anytime soon but the project has  received enough funding to start work  and complete the central valley portion  that runs through the agricultural  heartland of the golden state  in 2008 california voters approved a 10  billion dollar bond measure to provide  the initial funding to build the first  high-speed rail system in the united  states at that time the project was  estimated to cost about 40 billion  dollars  that cost has ballooned dramatically to  complete the entire section between san  francisco down to la with stops in san  jose and other major cities in  california the expectation is it's going  to run about 105 billion dollars and  that cost could still increase  currently the program does have funding  to complete 171 miles a portion through  the central valley of california  it's not heavily populated and it's one  of the easier sections to build through  and even that has been a very difficult  challenge to get going  already about 119 miles are under  construction running through cities  including fresno bakersfield and  eventually connecting up to merced in  the north  however to really make the system  meaningful it's going to have to have  those connections into the san francisco  bay area and down into los angeles in  southern california  so far the state of california has  provided more than nine billion dollars  for the project additionally  the  federal bipartisan infrastructure law  does provide some additional funding for  the program so between two and three  billion dollars are likely  to flow into the project to help get  that central valley portion completed in  total the expectation is building just  that first 171 miles will run between 23  and 25 billion dollars  many of those costs also include  securing some of the last remaining  sections of land needed in the central  valley  however an additional 80 billion dollars  is likely to be needed to do the much  more difficult work of getting to san  francisco and los angeles because the  system's going to have to bore through  major mountain ranges including the  tehachapi and san gabriel range in  southern california  and it will also have to contend with  earthquake fault lines that that ring  the state of california including the  san andreas fault  these are not insurmountable engineering  challenges they've been conquered in  places like japan which is equally  mountainous and also  highly prone to earthquakes but it does  add a lot of cost so it's very unclear  at this point exactly where all of that  additional money is going to come from  and what the timeline is to complete the  entire system the united states is a bit  of a laggard when it comes to high-speed  rail  america does have passenger rail in the  form of amtrak and some commuter lines  but compared to japan china  most of europe and a lot of other parts  of the world high-speed rail is really  not a factor here just yet  we are a car-based country in part  that's because the united states is so  very large so cars and airplanes tend to  be a bit more practical in most cases  but there is a movement that is  building to add a lot more high-speed  rail in the united states these are  incredibly expensive projects to do  money is not readily available and they  also take a very very long time to plan  out and do the actual construction work  proponents of the california system see  an opportunity to connect all of the  california cities including those in the  central valley that were bypassed by the  state's interstate 5 freeway the main  connector between southern california  and the bay area  travel time between los angeles to san  francisco when the system's completed  could be under three hours compared with  five to seven to eight hours by car  currently  ridership figures are estimated to be  about 50 million passengers annually by  2040  which could generate more than 3 billion  dollars of fair revenue  currently funding for the system  to build it is coming from california's  cap and trade program which is  generating about a billion dollars that  flows into  the california high-speed rail project's  annual budget how long those funds will  be available  isn't entirely clear the project does  have funding for the time being but the  big question is whether it can  ultimately find all of that additional  money that it's going to need to achieve  those big ridership numbers that are  anticipated  one of the benefits  that proponents also see is it will  really connect the state of california  in a way that it's not connected today  one could live in fresno or bakersfield  and commute to work in los angeles or up  into silicon valley  housing prices are astronomical in  southern and northern california much  more reasonable in the central valley so  it's a way in which there might be it  might make it easier for people to live  in in california a bit more affordably  what we're seeing right now is a little  bit of optimism for the system there's  been a lot of pessimism over the last  few years because of cost overruns and  the reality of how long this project is  going to take to complete the fact that  it's now able to move forward with this  first section does provide some optimism  that this might actually happen there's  been a lot of doubt that it would  however base best case scenario  the system won't be fully operational  until the late 2030s that first section  in the central valley  should begin operating by about 20 30  approximately eight years from now  however that's a lightly populated  section of california ridership numbers  aren't expected to be terribly high in  those early years not until it connects  into san jose san francisco and los  angeles in the south will california  begin to see really meaningful ridership  numbers but again everything hinges on  finding tens of billions of dollars that  currently do not exist in its budget
 

